<article class="env-definition" id="0061">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0061">Definition <span data-tag="0061">5.19.1</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a topological space. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $x, x' \in X$ then we say $x$ is a <em>specialization</em> of $x'$, or $x'$ is a <em>generalization</em> of $x$ if $x \in \overline{\{ x'\} }$. Notation: $x' \leadsto x$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A subset $T \subset X$ is <em>stable under specialization</em> if for all $x' \in T$ and every specialization $x' \leadsto x$ we have $x \in T$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A subset $T \subset X$ is <em>stable under generalization</em> if for all $x \in T$ and every generalization $x' \leadsto x$ we have $x' \in T$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>